"Workflows for work that runs the business Mistral AI has released 'Workflows', an orchestration layer for stable production transition of enterprise AI processes, as a public preview. Workflows is the orchestration layer that provides durability, observability, and fault tolerance for enterprise AI. Once developers write a workflow in Python, they can publish it to Le Chat for anyone in the organization to run, and every step is tracked and audited in Studio. The human approval step can be implemented in one line of code with functions like 'wait_for_input()', which waits for approval without consuming computational resources in a waiting state. Introducing Forge Mistral AI has launched Forge, a system that helps companies learn from their own proprietary data to build frontier AI models specialized for their business environment and internal knowledge. Forge lets you train AI models based on internal knowledge, including your company's engineering standards, compliance policies, codebase, and your organization's unique decision-making processes. It supports pre-training, post-training, and reinforcement learning stages throughout the model lifecycle to improve complex orchestration and tooling capabilities. We help companies in regulated industries securely introduce AI into their core systems and gain strategic autonomy while maintaining full control of their infrastructure and their own data sets. Mistral AI partners with NVIDIA to accelerate open frontier models Mistral AI has partnered with NVIDIA and will participate as a founding member of the 'NVIDIA Nemotron Coalition' to accelerate open, cutting-edge artificial intelligence models. Mistral AI plans to jointly develop a frontier open source AI model by utilizing NVIDIA's computing resources and synthetic data generation pipeline. The alliance's first initiative is a base model that will be learned on NVIDIA DGX Cloud and serve as the basis for the upcoming 'NVIDIA Nemotron 4' product line. As part of this collaboration, Mistral AI launched 'Mistral Small 4' to help developers, researchers, and enterprises build and innovate without barriers. Leanstral: Open-Source foundation for trustworthy vibe-coding Mistral AI has released ‘Leanstral’, the first open source code agent model to support Lean 4 attestation. Launch of Leanstral, a dedicated Lean 4 agent that can formally prove software specifications and mathematical objects to address human verification bottlenecks Designed with a highly sparse architecture of 6B active parameters to maximize efficiency and performance, and weights released under the Apache 2.0 license Demonstrated efficiency that surpasses the FLTEval performance of much larger open source models such as GLM5 and Kimi-K2.5 with just a single pass. Rails testing on autopilot: Building an agent that writes what developers won't Describes an example of building an autonomous AI agent that automatically writes and verifies Rails test code that developers are reluctant to write. Based on Vibe, Mistral's open source coding assistant, we build an autonomous agent that reads Rails source files and generates and improves RSpec tests. The agent analyzes five major file types (model, serializer, controller, mailer, and helper), writes tests for each type, and directly runs RSpec to verify syntax. Utilizes Factories and Fixtures for common context management, and uses mapping rules between source and spec files to identify test locations Jio Platforms files for India’s largest-ever IPO, with nearly $3 billion earmarked for debt repayment Jio Platforms, India's largest mobile operator, has filed for the largest initial public offering (IPO) in the country's history, raising around $3.8 billion to repay debt. Jio Platforms, a subsidiary of Mukesh Ambani's Reliance Industries, has filed preliminary prospectus (DRHP) with the Securities and Exchange Commission of India for an IPO worth about $3.8 billion. About $2.9 billion (Rs 275 billion) of the proceeds from the offering will be used to repay foreign currency-denominated external commercial borrowings (ECBs) of telecom arm Reliance Jio Infocomm. We plan to reduce financial costs through deleveraging through debt repayment and invest the secured financial capacity in 5G network advancement, fixed broadband expansion, AI, and cloud services. Wharton researchers coined ‘cognitive surrender’ to describe what happens when people let AI think for them An analysis of the 'cognitive surrender' phenomenon, in which humans rely on the results of artificial intelligence (AI) and follow AI's judgment without thinking or deciding on their own, and the apps that commercialized it Wharton School researchers discovered the phenomenon of ‘cognitive surrender’, in which people accept an answer 80% of the time even if the AI ​​provides an incorrect answer. By adding 'System 3', an AI-assisted cognitive method, to Daniel Kahneman's theory of thinking, he warned that human intuition and deliberation abilities could be weakened by entrusting thinking to AI. This phenomenon went beyond academic research and spread into everyday and commercial areas, leading to the launch of AI apps like ‘Moot’ that discuss and vote on users’ life decisions. The developer behind VLC’s 6 billion downloads now wants to connect hundreds of millions of robots Jean-Baptiste Kemp, lead developer of VLC media player, attracted $5 million in investment for Kyber, a startup developing an open source SDK that can control remote devices with ultra-low latency. Kyber, a startup founded by VLC lead developer Jean-Baptiste Kemp, attracted $5 million in seed investment led by Lightspeed. Kyber provides an SDK to control remote devices with extremely low latency (as low as 8 milliseconds) by synchronizing video, audio, sensor data, and control inputs. It targets robotics, drones, remote vehicles, cloud rendering, and remote IT access, and is already in commercial deployment for defense, communications, and robotics. How to Build a Forecasting Pipeline with TimeCopilot Using Foundation Models and Automated Anomaly Detection Provides a tutorial for building a time series prediction pipeline with a foundation model and automated anomaly detection using the TimeCopilot library. Install versions of NumPy and SciPy that are compatible with TimeCopilot and prepare your development environment. We construct a panel dataset by combining real airline passenger data and synthetic data injected with artificial anomalies. Set up a variety of statistical and foundational forecasting models, including AutoARIMA, AutoETS, Prophet, Chronos, and TimesFM, and evaluate the optimal model through cross-validation. Deontic Policies for Runtime Governance of Agentic AI Systems We propose AgenticRei, a deontic policy language and framework that provides runtime governance for autonomous agent systems based on large-scale language models (LLMs). Autonomous agent systems need to go beyond simple permission control and be constrained by complex corporate governance (allow, prohibit, mandate, suspend, etc.), but existing policy engines (XACML, Rego, Cedar, etc.) cannot fully support this. We propose AgenticRei, which combines an OWL expressive deontic policy language based on the Rei framework with a high-performance logic engine running outside of the LLM. This approach controls both tool invocation by agents and message exchange between agents, and can effectively define security and privacy governance constraints. Measuring Curriculum Alignment across Topical Coverage, Competency, and Cognitive Depth: A Longitudinal Framework Applied to CS2013 and CS2023 This study proposed and verified a human-in-the-loop pipeline that can measure and compare in a multidimensional manner how the curriculum of university computer science degree programs conforms to international standard guidelines (CS2013 and CS2023) and compare them over the long term. By building a pipeline combining semantic search and human verification, we found that computer science bachelor's programs cover approximately 50.9% and 49.7% of the knowledge units in the CS2013 and CS2023 guidelines, respectively. As a result of the search performance evaluation, the reciprocal rank fusion (RRF) ensemble model was the best, and the simple long-term context model performed worse than the small sentence embedding model, suggesting that measuring searcher selection is important. The rate of meeting the recommended cognitive depth suggested by the CS2023 guidelines was 76%, which was lower than 95% in CS2013. This is a result that reflects the raising of the standards in the new guidelines rather than a decrease in the quality of the program. Diffusion Language Models: An Experimental Analysis This study systematically analyzed the performance, computational efficiency, and inference factors of the diffusion language model (DLM), which has emerged as an alternative to the autoregressive model. We systematically experimentally analyze eight state-of-the-art diffusion language models (DLMs) across eight benchmarks. Identify the impact of key factors at inference time, such as denoising step, context length, block size, and parallel unmasking strategy, on model performance. We reveal that the behavior of DLMs is strongly influenced by design choices at the time of creation and has a distinct trade-off between performance and computational efficiency. Hidden Anchors in Multi-Agent LLM Deliberation This is a study that models and analyzes the impact of agents' individual internal beliefs (anchors) on opinion coordination in the multi-agent LLM consensus process using a closed-loop dynamic system. Like human decision-making, we modeled that LLM agents are continuously influenced by their own internal beliefs (anchors) as well as surrounding opinions. This hidden anchor can be restored only through the discussion process, which explains the phenomenon in which the agent's confidence level rises beyond the range of the initial opinion. As a result of testing three open source model groups, the strength of the anchor's influence was similar in all models, but the location of the anchor was different. DeXposure-Claw: An Agentic System for DeFi Risk Supervision We introduce DeXposure-Claw, a prediction-based agent system for credit risk supervision in decentralized finance (DeFi). To respond to rapid risks in the DeFi environment, we propose DeXposure-Claw, a prediction-based agent supervision system that solves the misjudgment problem of existing LLM agents. The system routes structured evidence through a graph time series foundation model (DeXposure-FM) that predicts future exposure networks, deterministic monitors, and data state gates to issue auditable supervisory tickets. To evaluate compliance with supervisory standards, we developed DeXposure-Bench, an evaluation system consisting of six axes, and proved its effectiveness with actual weekly data over five years. How many of the 170,000 English words do you know? Introduction and user feedback on VocabOwl, an English vocabulary testing service that estimates how many words a user actually knows VocabOwl is a tool that estimates the size of an individual's vocabulary through 100 questions based on 171,476 English words. A stratified sampling method was used and Gemini 3 Flash AI was used to create and operate vocabulary tests. Among users, ambiguity in the classification criteria for word difficulty, an unnecessarily high number of clicks, and easy elimination of incorrect answers according to view settings were pointed out. AI engineer claims to have cracked Linear A Tom Di Mino, a self-taught AI engineer, is being reviewed by the academic community for claiming to have deciphered Linear A, an undeciphered Bronze Age script, into a Semitic language. AI engineer Tom Di Mino has proposed a decipherment of the Minoan script Linear A, which has been undeciphered for over 100 years, and is being reviewed by Rutgers and Cambridge experts. Di Mino interpreted Linear A as an extinct Semitic language close to the predecessor of Biblical Hebrew and made a breakthrough by deciphering the key symbol *301 as 'na'. Using Claude Code, we queried and cross-referenced the GORILA and SigLA corpora to decipher 40 symbols, translate 408 terms, and draft a 9-page paper. The Illusion of Token Compression: Why to Be Skeptical of RTK A critical analysis of the fact that the RTK tool, which is said to reduce costs by compressing the terminal output of coding agents, has operational risks and limitations that reduce actual agent performance and accuracy. RTK's 60-90% savings figure is only a percentage of terminal output removal, not a reduction in overall API billing, and can cause silent failure of the agent due to loss of context information. There is a lack of strict SWE-bench-style task success rate evaluation, and attempts to save tokens may cause the agent to hallucinate or go into a loop, incurring more costs. It has a parsing structure that is vulnerable to changes in the output format of CLI tools such as git, cargo, and npm, so adding it as a synchronous external dependency to the production agent's critical path is high operational risk. The room that the economy cannot see Through the case of the Sverok gaming space in Stockholm, we highlight the value and necessity of third places and unpaid social activities that are not captured by economic price signals and market logic. Third places that reduce youth loneliness, such as the Sverok gaming space, create positive externalities, but their value is not reflected in the market as a price signal, making them difficult to sustain without subsidies. Unpaid activities that do not make money but consume time, such as caring, neighborly relationships, and running clubs, are decreasing due to labor and wage pressures. As wages become almost the only means of accessing housing and food, people are forced to do wage work of marginal value instead of more valuable unpaid activities. MCP용 Zero-Touch OAuth News of stabilization and initial support for the 'Enterprise-Managed Authorization (EMA)' extension, which allows companies to centrally manage MCP server permissions and users to access them at once without cumbersome consent procedures With the introduction of the EMA extension, enterprises can centrally control MCP server access rights through the organization's identity provider (IdP) instead of OAuth authorization on an individual user and server basis. Zero-Touch OAuth that can be used immediately after SSO login without additional consent screen is implemented using Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant (ID-JAG). In addition to Okta, Anthropic, and VS Code, major platforms such as Figma, Canva, and Supabase participated in the initial support of EMA. DVD-JEPA: an open-source, fully-reproducible JEPA world model [P] This is an introduction to DVD-JEPA, an open-source and reproducible mini-world model that implements a DVD logo-popping environment to visualize and verify the Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA). Unlike most world models, we demonstrate the JEPA structure, which discards unpredictable details by predicting future representations rather than pixels. By learning DVD logo bouncing data within a 16×16 grid, the exact (y, x) position of the logo is restored within 0.73 pixel error even without coordinate information. Through an optional decoder, future frames can be predicted and rendered for about 20 steps, and when an abnormal situation such as teleportation occurs, the error rapidly increases by 88 times compared to the baseline value, making it useful for detecting abnormalities. Time Series Modeling Needs a Dynamical Systems Perspective [R] It is argued that for the development of time series modeling, a dynamic system perspective should be introduced to understand the fundamental dynamic rules of the system beyond simple prediction and apply dynamic system reconstruction (DSR). We propose to focus on DSR-specific learning techniques and objectives, such as generalized teacher forcing, to reduce parameter capacity and complexity and capture long-term statistical characteristics when training time series models. By pre-learning a time series model through simulation of dynamic systems such as chaotic systems instead of artificially generated time series functions, more natural prior probabilities for real time series can be obtained. Since dynamical systems are defined by time recursion, we need to revert to modern RNNs instead of transformers to preserve the essential dynamic information of the time series. An open handbook on LLM inference at scale (GPU internals, KV cache, batching, vLLM/SGLang/TensorRT-LLM) [P] This is an open source handbook project that covers the internal structure of the LLM inference engine and the principles of GPU execution and memory operation. We analyze why GPUs primarily remain idle during LLM inference and how memory hierarchy affects throughput. Instead of text, use Mermaid diagrams to visually explain the architecture flow. Through GitHub, we are receiving feedback and contributions from developers with experience in inference in a production environment. Scrape Websites for Data without Webscraping Skills [P] AutomatiQ, a reverse engineering-based open source agent with no browser dependency, has been released to enable data collection without web scraping knowledge. After general web browsing, enter the required data and format into the agent, and a script is created to extract the data through reverse engineering. The generated script runs without running the browser, thus reducing additional CPU and memory costs and operating at high speed. It is free and open source that allows unlimited use of various artificial intelligence models and collection frameworks, including local models. Lighthouse agentic browsing scoring This is a guide to how Google Lighthouse's AI Agent-Friendly Website Browsing Performance Evaluation (Agentic Browsing) category works and evaluation factors. The Agentic Browsing category provides a pass/fail status and the percentage of agent readiness checks that passed, instead of a weighted average score from 0 to 100. It inspects the Accessibility Tree, which is key when the agent navigates a website, tool registration through the WebMCP API, Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS), and the presence of llms.txt. Dynamic tool registration timing, variability in accessibility tree construction due to changes in DOM size and complexity, layout shifts (CLS), etc. can affect Lighthouse measurement results. 4 easy tweaks you can make to your TV soundbar for more immersive audio How to enjoy immersive audio when watching sports games by simply adjusting your TV soundbar settings You can use the Room Calibration feature to optimize acoustics to suit the room structure and furniture arrangement. Activate dialogue or voice enhancements to hear the narrator clearly even over crowd noise or background noise. By activating night listening mode, you can hear conversations clearly even late at night without disturbing others. I made 7 changes to my Android Auto setup for better functionality when I'm driving For added convenience while driving, here are seven ways to customize Android Auto's app launcher, day/night mode, shortcuts, and notifications. You can expose only frequently used apps or arrange their order through ‘Launcher Customization’ in Android Auto settings. You can lock it in day or night mode, regardless of ambient brightness or time of day, or set it to follow your smartphone settings. You can add shortcuts and routines to the launcher to send texts to specific contacts or execute Gemini commands. War Taught this Ukrainian Entrepreneur the Value of Resilience This is the story of Salome Mikadze-Struk, a Ukrainian entrepreneur who protected the software development company Movadex despite the adversities of war and pandemic and raised awareness of the importance of resilience. While in college, I started Movadex using Ukrainian development talent during the COVID-19 pandemic. During Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022, the company stayed in business while taking online classes and evacuating employees from bomb shelters. After completing his MBA at Stanford University, he has been emphasizing the importance of entrepreneurial resilience in the rapidly changing software industry through AI coding tools through startup mentoring and lectures. Apple Launches Core AI for Apple-Silicon Optimized On-Device Generative AI At WWDC 26, Apple announced 'Core AI', a successor framework to Core ML that is optimized for Apple Silicon and enables running large-scale language models and generative AI on-device. Core AI, which runs exclusively on Apple Silicon, provides integrated hardware access and memory-safe Swift API and AOT compilation that seamlessly distributes work across CPU, GPU, and Neural Engine. PyTorch models can be converted to Core AI models through torch.export, and model compression techniques such as quantization and palletization can be applied for efficient on-device performance. With this announcement, Apple will support three different approaches to running machine learning and AI on its OS: Core ML, Core AI, and MLX Swift. Claude Fable 5 on Bedrock Requires Sharing Inference Data with Anthropic To use Anthropic's Claude Fable 5 and Mythos 5 models on Amazon Bedrock, you must share your inference data with Anthropic and allow it to be retained for 30 days. Using Claude Fable 5 or Mythos 5 on AWS Bedrock requires opt-in to data sharing mode (provider_data_share), which sends prompts and output to Anthropic for 30 days of retention and human review. Unlike all previous Bedrock models, data is transferred outside the AWS security perimeter to Anthropic, which can have a significant impact on companies' compliance and security screening. Anthropic explains that it is a safety requirement to detect new attacks and jailbreaks, but it is raising strong opposition and contractual concerns, especially in regulated industries such as European companies and healthcare/finance. Hackers Exploit Gravity SMTP WordPress Plugin Bug to Expose API Keys This is about a security incident in which hackers are stealing sensitive information such as API keys by exploiting a vulnerability in the Gravity SMTP WordPress plugin installed on approximately 100,000 websites. The vulnerability, CVE-2026-4020 (CVSS score 5.3), allows an unauthenticated attacker to extract a system report of approximately 365 KB in size containing sensitive information such as API keys, passwords, and OAuth tokens configured for email integration. The cause of the vulnerability is that the permission_callback of the REST API endpoint is incorrectly set to return true unconditionally, and a patch was provided in version 2.1.5 of the plugin. According to Wordfence, attacks have been detected since early May 2026, and peaked around June 6, 2026, peaking at more than 4 million requests per day, with a cumulative total of over 17 million attack attempts blocked. Everpure “Strengthening ‘data intelligence’ capabilities beyond simple storage” Everpure (formerly Pure Storage) has completed its name change and One Touch acquisition, and is launching a data intelligence and data-centric storage strategy that understands the meaning and context of data. Emphasizes the shift from application-centric to data-centric architecture that centers and references the data itself. Enhances data intelligence capabilities that go beyond storage to support security, semantics, and data cleansing. Through one-touch acquisition, we secure the ability to prevent duplication and risk based on semantic data and build an efficient RAG environment. MS increases profits by selling Open AI API to Chinese companies... ‘Distillation’ danger warning While Microsoft is making profits by providing open AI APIs to Chinese technology companies, controversy is rising about the risk of 'distillation' where Chinese companies imitate and learn AI technology. Microsoft has been providing large-scale open AI API services through Azure cloud to leading Chinese companies such as ByteDance. Although Microsoft's Azure AI business sales in China are rapidly increasing and it is recording great performance, the US political world and the AI ​​industry are concerned about national security and intellectual property leaks. OpenAI has expressed concern about blocking the 'distillation' practice of Chinese companies using model outputs to learn their own models, but Microsoft believes that it is difficult to completely prevent this technically. Perplexity launches ‘Brain’, an AI agent memory that learns on its own every night Perplexity has launched 'Brain', an agent memory system that remembers the agent's task performance process instead of the user's preferences and improves performance on its own. It is a task-centered memory system that breaks away from the existing user preference-centered memory and learns the agent's work process and success/failure experiences. It utilizes the 'Context Graph', which learns by comprehensively analyzing session records and document changes at night, like human sleep, and the 'LLM Wiki' structure, which is automatically updated. As a result of the initial internal evaluation, correct answer accuracy in similar tasks improved by 25%, recall improved by 16%, and related work costs decreased by 13%. Google and MS announce AI agent connection standard 'ARD'... Open AI and Antropic are in check Major IT companies such as Google, MS, and Salesforce have announced a new open standard called 'ARD' that supports automatic discovery and connection between AI agents, and have begun to keep OpenAI and Antropic's proprietary platform strategies in check. Google, MS, Salesforce, etc. participated to jointly promote ARD (Agentic Resource Discovery), a standard that connects different business apps and AI functions. ARD is a technical standard that allows various AI agents and business tools within a company to be automatically discovered, linked, and utilized in the backend. OpenAI and Antropic aim to be a 'super agent' platform centered on ChatGPT or Claude and are excluded from the list of participants in this ARD standard alliance. KAIST develops next-generation 'Omni RAG' technology that reduces AI hallucinations and increases speed 20 times KAIST researchers developed the next-generation 'Omni RAG' and integrated database technology 'Akashic DB', which reduce hallucinations of generative AI and increase processing speed by up to 20 times. We developed 'Akashic DB', which integrates the functions of vector DB, graph DB, and relational DB into one, and 'OmniRAG' technology based on it. By simultaneously searching and utilizing document meaning, object relationships, and structured data, complex queries that were difficult to solve with existing RAG were solved and hallucination problems were reduced. Compared to existing systems, answer accuracy has improved by up to 78%, and complex search query processing speed has been reduced by up to 20 times. [Axiological Society AX Column] Three misconceptions of manufacturing powerhouses in the AI ​​era In the AI ​​era, it is emphasized that Korean manufacturing companies must overcome three illusions in data collection, data export, and manufacturing AI introduction and properly utilize manufacturing data, which is their real weapon. Rather than focusing only on the data we have, we need to build a system like Tesla that collects data for new capabilities. We need to go beyond making products and create a business structure that collects and exports global data by adding a layer of intelligence on top of it. Rather than waiting to build a huge manufacturing AI system, we should start with a small model and use that solves a single problem in the field. MarkAny resolves prompt leakage issue with ‘AI DLP’ MarkAny has introduced a security solution that uses the endpoint agent method to block leakage of prompts to generative AI in real time, regardless of the network environment. As the use of generative AI increases, confidential leaks through prompt input windows, which are difficult to detect with existing file-based DLP, are becoming more frequent. MarkAny's Safe PC Enterprise controls prompts in real time even when offline or without a VPN connection through a single agent based on browser hooking. Detected blocking event records can be used as evidence to respond to security regulations such as the AI ​​Framework Act (enforced in January 2026) and the National Network Security System (N2SF)."